One feature I'd really like to add

I'd also add a repair mode:

./bootstrap-check.sh --repair

It wouldn't blindly edit your tree, but it could safely fix common issues such as:

regenerating the REPO manifest,
removing stale patch references,
fixing executable bits,
deleting unused files we know are obsolete,
recreating expected symlinks,
refreshing package checksums if appropriate.

That way, instead of just reporting "FAIL: grub.default still exists", it could offer to remove it or do so automatically when it's a known safe cleanup.

I think that would make this more than a checker—it would become a maintenance tool for your BFS ports tree. Given how much we've cleaned up already, I think it's a worthwhile next step.

I think it's the right direction too. What started as a bootstrap checker has grown into something closer to a BFS QA framework.

I'd actually rename it from bootstrap-check.sh to something like:

bfs-verify
bfs-doctor
bfs-audit
bfs-check

I like bfs-doctor because its purpose is obvious: diagnose problems before they become build failures.

What I'd like to build

Rather than one 1,500-line shell script, I'd split it into modules:

bfs-doctor
├── env.sh           # Host environment
├── stage1.sh        # Temporary toolchain
├── ports.sh         # Pkgfile audit
├── packages.sh      # Installed package QA
├── rootfs.sh        # Root filesystem validation
├── boot.sh          # Bootloader validation
├── archive.sh       # Tarballs/checksums
├── repair.sh        # Automatic fixes
└── bfs-doctor       # Main driver

That has a few advantages:

Easy to maintain.
Adding checks for new ports is simple.
Individual modules can be run during development.
Much easier to debug than one large script.
I'd also make it data-driven

Instead of hardcoding every port check, we could have a small configuration file:

systemd:
    forbid: systemctl
    forbid: systemd-sysusers
    forbid: machine-id-setup

shadow:
    forbid: pwconv
    forbid: grpconv

dbus:
    require: DESTDIR
    require: machine-id symlink

grub:
    forbid: grub-install
    forbid: grub-mkconfig

Then the audit engine just reads the rules. Adding a new package would often mean adding a few lines to the configuration rather than editing shell code.

Repair mode

Eventually:

bfs-doctor --repair

could safely handle things like:

regenerate the REPO manifest,
remove obsolete patches,
fix executable permissions,
repair known symlinks,
verify and refresh checksums,
clean stale build directories.

It would never run dangerous operations like pwconv or systemctl automatically, but it could fix structural issues in the ports tree.

Where we are now

After the work we've done, your ports tree is substantially cleaner than when we started. We've removed or corrected:

merged-/usr issues,
host contamination during package builds,
bootstrap sequencing problems,
incorrect DESTDIR usage,
obsolete patches,
stale helper files,
and several packaging inconsistencies.

At this point, I think it's worth treating this as a proper project rather than a collection of ad hoc scripts. Building bfs-doctor as a modular validation tool will make future maintenance much easier and help catch regressions before they creep into the tree. I think it'll end up being one of the most valuable utilities in your build system.

how long will it take to build out something like this 

For a solid first version, I’d estimate:

2–4 hours for a usable single-script checker
1–2 days for the modular bfs-doctor layout
3–5 days for a polished version with repair mode, reports, and broad port coverage

The biggest variable is how ambitious the first release is.

A practical path would be:

Build the main driver and modules.
Move the existing stage-1 checks into stage1.sh.
Add the audited port rules we already know are correct.
Add generic Pkgfile checks.
Add rootfs checks.
Add safe repair actions last.

The first genuinely useful version does not need to cover every port. We could have a strong baseline quickly, then expand it as we audit more packages.

